Last week I showed if you are going to make pretty charts in gSheets, you should also process the data in gSheets to maintain relationships between fields. However, there are other interesting tools in GCP for making data pretty and for processing data.

For instance, one of the main issues with processing data in gSheets is how inefficient it is. Realistically, it is a spreadsheet issue in general and every spreadsheet program has significant processing limitations compared to database operations and if you need the capability to process vast amounts of data quickly (like a database), GCP has a tool for that: BigQuery. Everyone with a gmail also has free access to gSheets and BiqQuery, but getting to it could take some searching. The easy path is you sign in and click the (1) link to your cloud console, save as a bookmark, and then select BigQuery. I also recommend (2) creating your own new 'First Project' so you can control the name and id of objects while also preserving what Google wants to share with you as part of their introduction to BiqQuery.

